  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct attribute to use is <strong>UpdateReplacePolicy</strong>. This resource attribute controls what happens to the existing resource when it is replaced during a CloudFormation stack update. Setting <strong>UpdateReplacePolicy</strong> to <code>Retain</code> ensures that the old DB instance is preserved instead of being deleted.<br><br><em>UpdatePolicy</em> is not correct; it's used for resources like Auto Scaling groups or EFS, and handles how certain updates are performed, not the retention of replaced resources.<br><br><em>DeletionPolicy</em> only affects what happens when the stack or the individual resource is deleted, not during replacements on updates.<br><br><em>CreationPolicy</em> is used to signal resource creation completion, and doesn't impact what happens to existing resources when a replacement is needed.<br><br><strong>Tip:</strong> Always use <strong>UpdateReplacePolicy</strong> if you need to manage what CloudFormation does to replaced resources during stack updates. <br><br>In summary, choose <strong>UpdateReplacePolicy</strong> to ensure the old DB instance is retained during a resource replacement.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have defined an <strong>AWS::RDS::DBInstance</strong> resource in a CloudFormation stack. Changes to the stack are applied using a Jenkins pipeline whenever updates are made to the CloudFormation template. If an update to the stack requires replacing the DB instance, you want to guarantee that the original (old) DB instance is retained after the new one is successfully created. <br> Which specific CloudFormation attribute should be set on the DB instance resource to accomplish this behavior?</p>",
